  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 151 AM MDT Fri Aug 21 2026\n\nAnother hot day is on tap to end the week as upper-level ridging\ncontinues to dominate the weather pattern, supporting above-normal\ntemperatures across the forecast area. Afternoon high \ntemperatures will range from the mid to upper 90s across the \nplains, with a few locations reaching 100 degrees. Aside from the\nheat, the main concerns for today will be with the development of\nisolated to scattered high-based showers and storms as \nnorthwesterly flow aloft allows some mid-level Pacific moisture \nto sneak under the amplifying ridge. Expecting convection to \nstart over the higher elevations in the afternoon and \nshowers/storms to move southeast onto the lower elevations, where \ndeep evaporative cooling will likely lead to microbursts and gusty\noutflows, with little to no precipitation expected. Gusts between\n45 to 55 mph will be possible along the northern portion of the urban\ncorridor where DCAPE is expected to range from 1300-1700 J/kg. \nFarther east, a few strong to severe storms will be possible \nacross our northeast corner in the evening, where slightly \nstronger northwest flow, low-level moisture, and instability will \nbe in place. While the more robust storms are expected to stay to \nour northeast, we can't rule out seeing a few severe gusts or \nhailstones with any stronger storm that develops. The SPC has \nissued a Marginal Risk for these hazards for our northeast corner,\nwhich seems reasonable at this time.\n\nLooking into the weekend, the ridge is expected to persist, with a\ngradual eastward progression each day. Despite the ridge being\noverhead, mid-level moisture is expected to increase. Ensembles \ncontinue to show above normal PWAT values through the weekend, \nwith the peak expected on Saturday. Surface dewpoints along with\ninstability both increase on Saturday that will allow scattered to\nnumerous high-based showers and storms to develop in the \nafternoon, and with forecast soundings only showing small \nimprovements to DCAPE values, storms will be capable of producing\ngusty outflows and microbursts once again. Can't rule out a few \nstrong to severe gusts with the strongest storms. With regards to\nQPF, Saturday looks to be our best chance for more widespread\naccumulating rainfall (a few tenths), with a few localized \nheavier totals possible. Afternoon high temperatures will cool by\nfive to ten degrees, with 80s and 90s still expected across the\nplains and mountain valleys. Afternoon showers and storms are\nexpected again on Sunday, though coverage is expected to decrease\ncompared to Saturday, with the higher elevations having the best \nchance for a few hundredths to around a tenth of an inch. Another\nday of afternoon high temps in the 90s is expected across the\nplains, with a slight cooldown for the mountain valleys. \n\nA shortwave passing over the Northern Rockies on Monday is\nexpected to flatten the ridge for a day or two, bringing a slight\ncool down for mid-week. Afternoon showers and storms are expected\nto persist each afternoon through the forecast period, with the\nNBM pointing to another warming and drying trend returning by the\nsecond half of the week.",
